WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.915
Theo markets a group plan to Priya and

00:00:02.915 --> 00:00:05.465
then helps her explain it to employees.

00:00:05.465 --> 00:00:09.108
The same truth-in-advertising rules apply: materials must be accurate, name

00:00:09.108 --> 00:00:12.387
the insurer, and present limitations as clearly as benefits.

00:00:12.387 --> 00:00:16.030
Enrollment meetings, brochures and the certificate itself are advertising for

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:19.309
this purpose, and the insurer is responsible for them.

00:00:19.309 --> 00:00:22.223
Employer communications that oversell the plan can create

00:00:22.223 --> 00:00:25.138
liability for benefits the plan does not provide.

00:00:25.138 --> 00:00:28.781
The summary of benefits and coverage uses a standard format

00:00:28.781 --> 00:00:32.060
precisely so employees can compare plans without marketing spin.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.150
Group contracts cross state lines.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:39.584
The general rule is that the law of the state where the master

00:00:39.584 --> 00:00:44.600
policy is delivered governs the contract, even if certificate holders live elsewhere.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.944
An employer headquartered in Delaware with a Maryland

00:00:47.944 --> 00:00:50.870
branch may hold a Delaware master policy.

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:55.468
Maryland protects its residents by extending certain mandated benefits and consumer

00:00:55.468 --> 00:01:00.066
protections to certificates delivered to Maryland residents under out-of-state group policies,

00:01:00.066 --> 00:01:03.828
and by requiring out-of-state carriers to be authorized here.

00:01:03.828 --> 00:01:08.008
When a question asks which state’s rules apply, start with

00:01:08.008 --> 00:01:11.770
the place of delivery, then check for extraterritorial provisions.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:16.466
An insurer underwriting Priya’s bakery does not examine twenty people.

00:01:16.466 --> 00:01:18.344
It examines the group.

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:23.979
Characteristics of the group include its size, industry, age and gender mix,

00:01:23.979 --> 00:01:29.614
location and turnover; a stable workforce in a low-hazard industry prices well.

00:01:29.614 --> 00:01:33.841
Plan design matters: rich benefits with no employee contribution

00:01:33.841 --> 00:01:38.067
invite heavier use than a plan with cost sharing.

00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:42.293
Persistency, the group’s history of staying with carriers, signals

00:01:42.293 --> 00:01:46.050
whether the insurer will recover its setup costs.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:52.624
Administrative capability, the employer’s ability to handle payroll deductions, enrollments and records, affects expense.

00:01:52.624 --> 00:01:57.790
For small groups, health status may not be used at all.

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:01.690
The insurer requires a minimum share of eligible employees to enroll.

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:06.653
In a noncontributory plan, where the employer pays the entire premium, 100 percent of

00:02:06.653 --> 00:02:11.262
eligible employees must participate, because there is no reason for anyone to decline.

00:02:11.262 --> 00:02:15.871
In a contributory plan, where employees pay part, carriers typically require about 75

00:02:15.871 --> 00:02:20.126
percent of eligible employees who do not have other coverage to enroll.

00:02:20.126 --> 00:02:24.026
The rule prevents a plan from enrolling only the sickest employees.

00:02:24.026 --> 00:02:28.280
Priya’s plan is contributory; she needs at least fifteen of twenty eligible

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:32.180
employees to join, not counting those covered by a spouse’s plan.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:36.860
Carriers also require the employer to contribute a minimum share of

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:41.539
the premium, commonly at least 50 percent of the employee-only cost.

00:02:41.539 --> 00:02:45.793
The contribution makes enrollment attractive to healthy employees, which keeps

00:02:45.793 --> 00:02:49.622
the risk pool balanced and the participation requirement reachable.

00:02:49.622 --> 00:02:54.727
Maryland’s small group market and the SHOP marketplace allow carriers to set

00:02:54.727 --> 00:02:59.832
reasonable participation and contribution requirements, applied uniformly, and to decline a small

00:02:59.832 --> 00:03:04.937
group that fails them, even though they may never decline for health.

00:03:04.937 --> 00:03:09.191
A small business tax credit may offset part of the

00:03:09.191 --> 00:03:13.020
employer’s share for qualifying employers that buy through SHOP.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.877
Every rule in this lesson fights the same enemy: adverse selection.

00:03:17.877 --> 00:03:21.850
Advertising rules keep employees from buying on false expectations.

00:03:21.850 --> 00:03:26.265
Jurisdiction rules make sure a state’s protections follow its residents.

00:03:26.265 --> 00:03:31.563
Group underwriting prices the whole group fairly without singling out sick employees.

00:03:31.563 --> 00:03:37.303
Participation and contribution requirements make sure the healthy join along with the sick.

00:03:37.303 --> 00:03:41.718
Theo tells Priya that meeting the participation rule is not

00:03:41.718 --> 00:03:46.133
bureaucracy; it is what keeps her premium affordable next year.

00:03:46.133 --> 00:03:50.106
Lesson 37 turns to the employees themselves: who is

00:03:50.106 --> 00:03:54.080
eligible, when they may enroll and how benefits coordinate.
